# the argument of hume-treatise-2.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
atom	convinced	men are once fully convinced of the two principles: that nothing in any object, considered in itself, can afford a reason for drawing a conclusion beyond it, and that even after observing frequent or constant conjunction we have no reason to infer beyond what we have experienced
atom	loose	men are thrown loose from all common systems
atom	receive_extraordinary	men make no difficulty of receiving any system, however extraordinary it may appear
atom	convincing_causal	the two principles have been found sufficiently convincing even with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation
atom	evident_probable	with regard to conjectural or probable reasonings the two principles acquire a new degree of evidence
atom	vulgar_imperfect	every vulgar hypothesis on this subject is imperfect
atom	readers_reject	the generality of readers will reject as chimerical whatever does not agree with commonly received notions
